A||||11||LS1swap||where to get KYB springs and struts???||||5||
Z||000000||LS1swap||09-04-2001||06:38 PM||||Other than online is there any auto parts store that carry the KYB springs and struts? such as track or autozone.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Uaana||09-05-2001||04:49 AM||||You should be able to get them at almost any auto parts store only problem is the mark up.<BR>I bought my KYB struts from shockwarehouse.com for about 45 each. <BR>When I checked at Champion they were 100 each.<BR>It's one of the few things where putting up with the delay is definatly worth it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula88||09-05-2001||10:14 AM|||| <A HREF="http://www.tirerack.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.tirerack.com</A>  has KYB cheaper than shockwarehouse.com.  Only a few dollars difference, but depending on shipping, you could save a bit more.||||reg||6
Z||000003||gargoyle||09-05-2001||10:23 AM||||I just checked The Tire Rack and they would cost you 58 pair(front), 104 pair rear. Also if you went with the Eibach springs (262.00) the whole package shipped to you would be 453.49. I have dealt wi th them several times, they are really good.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000004||Alex4mula||09-05-2001||10:33 AM||||Does TireRack sells KYBs and springs for 88s? TIA||||reg||1
Z||000005||Formula88||09-05-2001||10:54 AM||||Tire Rack has front KYB shocks for 88's.  They don't show a listing for 88 rear struts, but from what I've heard, they're the same from 84-88, so you can use 87 struts, which they do carry.  I noted that the Fiero Store lists one part number for KYB rear struts for 84-88.  Tire Rack has the GR-2 and Gas-A-Just shocks for the front, and GR-2 struts for the rear.<P>I plan on going with the Gas-A-Just fronts with the GR-2 rears on my Formula.  You'll save about $60, I think, over the Fiero Stores' prices for a set of 4.||||reg||6
Z||000006||LS1swap||09-05-2001||08:46 PM||||Thanks everyone sounds good . but I am not sure about the lowering springs. I have to be careful as it is with my GT not to bottom out. Thanks again.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Formula88||09-05-2001||09:00 PM||||Well, the KYB shocks and struts are good for a stock ride height.  The only lowering springs  I know of for 88s are the Fiero Store's.  You can get Eibach for pre 88's, and I'm sure some others.  But the KYB's should work with any of them.||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfiero||09-06-2001||07:50 PM||||If you have an '88 Fiero and order from the Tire Rack over the phone, just tell them you have 2 Fieros......one '88 that needs front shocks and one '87 that needs rear struts.  Believe me, you'll save some hassles.<P>I just tried ordering a set of KYB's for my '88 GT online, but their website wasn't working for me so I called them up.  I told the guy I needed a set of KYB GR-2 shocks for an '88 and a set of KYB GR-2 struts for an '87.  He asked if that was it.....and why not get the other stuff for each.  That's when I made my mistake.  I said the struts were going on my '88.....he began to "teach" me the differences between the '84-'87 and the '88 suspensions (HA!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ). <P>At any rate.....even after telling him that I have owned over 2 dozen '88 Fieros and that this was my 6th set of KYBs going on an '88 he still didn't get it.  He then said that I wouldn't be able to return them if they didn't fit......to that I said "That'll be just fine with me!".   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>It was an ordeal to say the least.  I guess that's why I like the impersonal nature of the internet.......you can order one shock for a Fiero, another for a Yugo and yet another for a Honda Civic and it won't ask you why.  The computer just accepts that you know what you're doing and takes the order.<P>But I digress...... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||Formula88||09-06-2001||08:23 PM||||Actually, I'm impressed that they took that stance.  Them knowing their's a difference between 84-7 and 88 is impressive.  I can forgive them not knowing the 87 struts will work on the 88.  I wasn't even sure about that till reading this forum.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Formula88||09-06-2001||08:25 PM||||BTW, how do you like those GR2's on the front of your Fiero?  I was thinking about going with the Gas-A-Just on the front and the GR2's on the back.  I'd do Gas-A-Just all the way around if I could.  Have you tried the Gas-A-Just up front?||||reg||1
Z||000011||mrfiero||09-06-2001||08:32 PM||||Well, OK.......maybe it's a good thing they know the differences.  He did say "the '84-'87s have a Chevette front end and a Citation rear"......that is a little inaccurate, but is as common a mistake as the Lotus designed '88 suspension.<P><BR>I tried the Gas-A-Just fronts, but found them to be too stiff for my GT at the time (lowered springs & poly bushings).  I went with new rubber busghings on my current GT, so maybe they wouldn't be as harsh (I wasn't willing to pay to find out!).<P>The GR-2's do a good enough job for me and I have been satisfied with them over the years.  For $16 apiece you can't go wrong (Gas-A-Justs were $29). ||||reg||1
